($q$Super lemon juice$q$, $q$keeps$q$, $q$https://hildaskitchenblog.com/recipe/how-to-make-super-juice$q$, $q$reference$q$, DATE '2026-10-13', $q$Keep for Hilda's own lemon recipe (same ratios): blending the peels, as we do, cuts it to 1 week.$q$),
($q$Super lemon juice$q$, $q$keeps$q$, $q$https://pinestrawmag.com/in-the-spirit-50/$q$, $q$reference$q$, DATE '2026-10-13', $q$Keep for Pinestraw's own recipes (same lemon ratio): all at their best for the first week, turning metallic and bitter after.$q$),
($q$Super lime juice$q$, $q$recipe$q$, $q$https://hildaskitchenblog.com/recipe/how-to-make-super-juice$q$, $q$reference$q$, DATE '2026-10-13', $q$Credits Nickle Morris. For lime, peel weight x 0.66 citric acid and x 0.33 malic acid, then water at 16.66 x peel weight; acids rest on the peels 1 to 2 hours; juice of the peeled fruit added. Matches ours per 100 g peel.$q$),
($q$Super lime juice$q$, $q$recipe$q$, $q$https://vinepair.com/articles/what-is-super-juice-citrus/$q$, $q$reference$q$, DATE '2026-10-13', $q$Credits Nickle Morris. Acids equal to the peel weight, two parts citric to one malic; about 8 limes for roughly a litre. Agrees with our acid amounts.$q$),
($q$Super lime juice$q$, $q$recipe$q$, $q$https://pinestrawmag.com/in-the-spirit-50/$q$, $q$reference$q$, DATE '2026-10-13', $q$Eight limes give about 100 g of peel and almost 8 oz (about 240 ml) of juice, which supports our 250 ml. Its lime acid ratio (0.4 citric, 0.3 malic) differs; we use the 0.66 and 0.33 above.$q$),
